{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Ice Shelf Polynya: Diatom Bloom Over an Antarctic Sponge Ground",
    "date": "2026-09-12",
    "lesson": "A coastal Antarctic polynya opens each spring when wind pushes sea ice offshore, letting light flood a water column that has been dark all winter. Diatoms bloom explosively in the surface layer while their sinking cells and exudates feed a bacterial community below, including chitin- and glycan-degrading heterotrophs that make a living decomposing frustules and detritus. Deeper still, near the seafloor, sponge-associated and free-living bacteria scavenge whatever settles past the bloom. This level asks you to survive as a heterotrophic decomposer riding the pulse of carbohydrate-rich diatom fallout, in cold water where every metabolic step is slowed by Q10, while pennate diatoms glide over the sediment surface and centric diatoms sink past you in chains.",
    "citation": "Cape Bathurst / McMurdo Sound polynya spring bloom studies; Antarctic benthic-pelagic coupling literature.",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Antarctic coastal polynya spring diatom bloom and benthic-pelagic carbon coupling"
  },
